# Lesson 24: Phrasal Verbs and Collocations

> A phrasal verb is a verb plus a particle whose meaning is often more than
> the sum of its parts, while a collocation is the company a word habitually
> keeps. Exams love both: error-spotting questions hide wrong particles, and
> vocabulary questions test make-versus-do pairs. This lesson builds both
> skills with tables you can revise the night before the exam.

## 1. What Is a Phrasal Verb?

[definition]
A **phrasal verb** is a combination of a verb and one or more particles
(an adverb, a preposition, or both) that functions as a single verb with
a meaning that is frequently **idiomatic** — not predictable from the
individual words alone.
[/definition]

* *I **ran into** an old friend.* — here *ran into* means "met by chance",
  not literally running.
* The three technical terms you need: the **verb** (the head word), the
  **particle** (the adverb or preposition that follows), and the
  **object** (the noun or pronoun the phrase acts upon, if any).
* Phrasal verbs are extremely common in spoken English and in reading
  passages; formal writing prefers their Latinate single-word equivalents
  (*postpone* instead of *put off*).

@diagram tree id="phrasal-verb-types" layout="tree" aria="Classification tree of phrasal verbs"
  "Phrasal verbs" -> "Intransitive (no object)"
  "Phrasal verbs" -> "Transitive (takes an object)"
  "Intransitive (no object)" -> "The plane took off at dawn."
  "Intransitive (no object)" -> "Prices went up again."
  "Transitive (takes an object)" -> "Separable: turn the light off"
  "Transitive (takes an object)" -> "Inseparable: look after the baby"
  "Transitive (takes an object)" -> "Three-word: run out of patience"
@/diagram

[tip]
Ask one question first: *does it take an object?* Only transitive phrasal
verbs can be separated, and only separable ones allow a pronoun in the
middle. Answering this one question resolves half of all phrasal-verb
exam items instantly.
[/tip]

## 2. Separable and Inseparable Phrasal Verbs

| Type | Rule | Example | Pronoun test |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Separable | object may come between verb and particle, or after | turn **off** the light / turn the light **off** | turn *it* off |
| Inseparable | object must follow the complete phrase | look **after** the baby | look after *her* |
| Three-word | always inseparable; ends in a preposition | put **up with** the noise | put up with *it* |

The pronoun test is the fastest diagnostic. With a separable verb the
pronoun *must* sit in the middle: *turn it off* is correct, *turn off it*
is wrong. With an inseparable verb the reverse holds: *look after her* is
correct, and *look her after* is impossible.

[warning]
With separable verbs and a **long noun-phrase object**, keep the object
after the particle: *call off the meeting scheduled for Friday* is far
better than *call the meeting scheduled for Friday off*. Exams mark the
middle placement of long objects as clumsy or wrong.
[/warning]

## 3. Three-Word Phrasal Verbs

Three-word phrasal verbs combine a verb, an adverb, and a preposition.
They are always inseparable, and the final word is a preposition that
must be followed by an object.

| Three-word phrasal verb | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| look forward to | anticipate with pleasure | I look forward to **meeting** you. |
| put up with | tolerate | She refuses to put up with rudeness. |
| get away with | escape punishment for | He got away with cheating once. |
| run out of | exhaust the supply of | We have run out of time. |
| catch up with | reach the same level as | He soon caught up with the class. |
| keep up with | stay level with | Walk slowly; I cannot keep up with you. |
| be up to | be doing (often mischief); be one's duty | What are the children up to? |
| look up to | admire | Students look up to sincere teachers. |
| look down on | despise | Never look down on the poor. |
| get on with | have a friendly relationship with | Do you get on with your neighbours? |

[important]
After the *to* of *look forward to*, use a **gerund**, not a base verb:
*I look forward to hearing from you* — never *to hear*. The *to* here is
a preposition, and prepositions take the $V_1 + ing$ form (see Lesson 18).
[/important]

## 4. One Verb, Many Meanings: Particle Shifts

The same verb changes meaning completely when the particle changes.
Exam setters exploit exactly this: they put a correct-looking verb with
the wrong particle.

| Verb | With different particles | Meanings |
| --- | --- | --- |
| break | break down / break out / break into / break up | stop working / escape or begin suddenly / enter by force / end a relationship |
| get | get over / get on / get off / get up / get through | recover / board or progress / alight / rise from bed / survive or pass |
| take | take off / take after / take up / take in / take back | remove or leave the ground / resemble / begin a hobby / deceive or grasp / withdraw |
| turn | turn down / turn up / turn out / turn into | refuse / arrive or increase volume / prove to be / become |
| call | call off / call on / call for / call up | cancel / visit or appeal to / demand / telephone or recall |
| set | set up / set out / set in / set aside | establish / begin a journey / begin and persist / reserve or reject |

[example]
Choose the correct option: *The meeting was (a) called off (b) called on
(c) called for owing to the strike.*
Step 1: the sense required is "cancelled".
Step 2: *call off* = cancel, so the answer is (a) called off.
Step 3: *called on* would mean "visited", *called for* would mean
"demanded" — both leave the sentence meaningless.
[/example]

## 5. High-Frequency Phrasal Verbs: Three Tables

**Table A (A to D)**

| Phrasal verb | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| account for | explain; constitute | Laziness accounts for his failure. |
| back up | support; make a copy | Evidence backed up her claim. |
| blow up | explode; inflate | The bridge was blown up in 1943. |
| break down | stop functioning | The bus broke down midway. |
| break out | begin suddenly | Cholera broke out in the camp. |
| bring up | rear; mention | She brought up the issue again. |
| call off | cancel | They called off the match. |
| carry on | continue | Carry on with your work. |
| carry out | perform; execute | The lab carried out the test. |
| come across | meet by chance | I came across an old diary. |
| cut down on | reduce | Cut down on sugar. |
| deal with | handle | The committee deals with complaints. |
| do away with | abolish | The Act was done away with. |

**Table B (E to P)**

| Phrasal verb | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| figure out | understand | I cannot figure out this map. |
| find out | discover | Find out the truth yourself. |
| get along with | be friendly with | He gets along with everyone. |
| get away with | escape penalty | No one gets away with fraud here. |
| get over | recover from | She got over the fever. |
| give in | yield | The rebel never gave in. |
| give up | abandon | Never give up hope. |
| go through | examine; endure | The auditor went through every file. |
| look after | take care of | Nurses look after patients. |
| look down on | despise | Do not look down on juniors. |
| look forward to | anticipate eagerly | We look forward to the results. |
| look into | investigate | Police will look into the matter. |
| make up | compose; reconcile; invent | She made up a story. |
| pass away | die | The veteran passed away peacefully. |
| put off | postpone | Put off the trip to Monday. |
| put up with | tolerate | He cannot put up with noise. |

**Table C (P to Z)**

| Phrasal verb | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| run out of | exhaust the supply | The ink has run out. |
| set up | establish | They set up a free clinic. |
| settle down | become calm or established | He settled down in Pune. |
| show off | boast | Peacocks show off their feathers. |
| stand for | represent; tolerate | CBI stands for Central Bureau of Investigation. |
| take after | resemble a relative | Ravi takes after his father. |
| take off | leave the ground; remove | The flight took off on time. |
| turn down | reject | She turned down the offer. |
| turn up | arrive unexpectedly | He turned up an hour late. |
| wear out | become useless through use | Cheap shoes wear out fast. |
| work out | solve; exercise; succeed | Things will work out well. |
| fall through | fail to happen | The deal fell through. |

That is more than forty verbs; revise them in three sittings of one
table each, covering the example column and testing yourself.

## 6. Collocations: Make versus Do and Beyond

[definition]
A **collocation** is a pair or group of words that habitually occur
together: *heavy rain*, not *strong rain*; *strong tea*, not *powerful
tea*. Collocation errors sound wrong to a native ear even when the
grammar is perfect.
[/definition]

The classic exam pair is **make** (create, produce, construct) versus
**do** (perform an activity or duty):

| Make ... | Do ... |
| --- | --- |
| make a mistake | do homework |
| make a decision | do business |
| make an effort | do a favour |
| make progress | do one's duty |
| make an excuse | do damage |
| make money | do justice to |
| make noise | do a good job |
| make arrangements | do research |

[warning]
*Do* concerns the **activity**, *make* concerns the **result**. You *do*
an experiment but *make* a discovery. Mixing these two is the single most
frequent collocation error in Indian exam scripts.
[/warning]

## 7. Adjective, Adverb, and Verb Collocations

| Pattern | Correct collocation | Wrong version |
| --- | --- | --- |
| adjective + noun | heavy rain, heavy traffic | strong rain |
| adjective + noun | strong tea, strong wind | heavy tea |
| adjective + noun | fast asleep, fast food | quick asleep |
| adjective + noun | bitter cold, bitter disappointment | cool disappointment |
| adverb + adjective | bitterly cold, fully aware | fully cold |
| adverb + adjective | widely known, deeply hurt | widely hurt |
| verb + noun | catch a cold, catch fire | take a cold |
| verb + noun | break the news, break a promise | cut the news |
| verb + noun | pay attention, pay a compliment | give attention |
| verb + noun | keep a promise, keep quiet | fulfil quiet |
| verb + noun | save time, take a risk | spend a risk |
| noun + noun | a bar of soap, a burst of applause | a piece of applause |

