## 8. Exam Error Patterns and Practice

Error-spotting questions (see also Lesson 26) hide phrasal-verb faults in
five predictable ways:

1. **Wrong particle**: *He compensated for the loss* is right, but
   *He compensated the loss* is wrong — the fixed partner *for* was
   dropped.
2. **Illegal separation**: *look the word after* for *look after* — an
   inseparable verb forced apart.
3. **Pronoun stranded at the end**: *turn off it* instead of *turn it off*.
4. **Register clash**: *The Minister put off the summit* — in formal
   writing prefer *postponed*; phrasal verbs are informal.
5. **Gerund after *to***: *I look forward to hear* instead of
   *to hearing*.

[example]
Spot the error: *She could not (a) put up (b) with the (c) insult any longer.*
The three-word verb *put up with* is inseparable, and the sentence
splits it correctly — so there is **no error** here. Exams do include
error-free sentences, so never invent a fault to seem diligent.
[/example]

[tip]
When two options differ only in the particle, translate the sentence
with each candidate meaning: *break down* (stop working) versus
*break out* (begin suddenly) — the surrounding words (*bus*, *epidemic*)
immediately select the right partner. Context is the answer key.
[/tip]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Cue | Phrasal verb or collocation | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| cancel (formal: postpone alternative) | call off | cancel |
| recover from | get over | overcome |
| investigate | look into | inquire into |
| tolerate | put up with | bear |
| resemble (a parent) | take after | look like |
| reject politely | turn down | refuse |
| exhaust a supply | run out of | finish |
| established pair | heavy rain | not strong rain |
| established pair | strong tea | not powerful tea |
| activity verb | do homework, do a favour | perform |
| result verb | make a mistake, make progress | produce |

[summary]
A phrasal verb = verb + particle acting as one verb; it is transitive or
intransitive, and only separable transitives accept a pronoun in the
middle, while three-word verbs are never separated. One verb takes many
particles with different meanings, so the context selects the partner.
Collocations are fixed habits — make a mistake, do homework, heavy rain,
strong tea — and exam errors cluster around wrong particles, illegal
splitting, and gerunds after *look forward to*.
[/summary]
